<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  time_trials.launch.xml - drive the saved raceline, alone, no obstacles.

  Sits on top of localization.launch.xml, which it includes. Pick a map that
  has been through raceline_generator and the car follows its global waypoints.

      ros2 launch stack_master time_trials.launch.xml map:=<name>

  The driving half of the stack, with the obstacle half optional:

      state_machine     /global_waypoints_scaled + /global_waypoints/overtaking
                        -> /behavior_strategy, /local_waypoints
      controller_manager that -> /vesc/high_level/ackermann_cmd
      simple_mux        arbitrates controller vs joystick -> /vesc/ackermann_cmd

  obstacles:=true adds static obstacle avoidance:

      detect       /scan            -> /detect/raw_obstacles
      tracking     those            -> /tracking/obstacles     (sets is_static)
      spline_node  those            -> /planner/avoidance/static_otwpnts

  It also flips timetrials_only off, which is the switch that makes the state
  machine look at obstacles at all - with it on, obstacle_perception_cb returns
  immediately and the car stays in RACELINE no matter what is in the way. The
  two always move together, so obstacles is the only argument.

  THE REMAP IS THE POINT. spline_node publishes /planner/avoidance/otwpnts, the
  overtaking topic, and the state machine only reaches that through
  _check_ot_sector - which needs overtaking sectors in the map's
  ot_sectors.yaml, and a map that has none can never leave TRAILING. It would
  slow down behind an obstacle and sit there. The static path
  (_check_static_overtaking_mode) asks for no sector, only that the car is
  under 3 m/s and closing, and it reads /planner/avoidance/static_otwpnts. In
  time trials every obstacle is static, so that is what these waypoints are.

  Two things to expect when watching it work. The state reads OVERTAKE either
  way - static and dynamic avoidance enter the same state and differ only in
  which waypoints they follow - so watch /planner/avoidance/static_otwpnts to
  tell them apart. And avoidance only arms below 3 m/s, so approaching an
  obstacle fast gives TRAILING first: the car closes, slows, and swings out
  once it is under the threshold and still closing. That order is deliberate.

  Not started, and not needed here: the dynamic planner in spline_planner
  (it waits on /ot_section_check, which an empty ot_sectors.yaml never grants)
  and everything under prediction. Both belong to head-to-head.

  qual:=true is the race-day qualifying run:

      ros2 launch stack_master time_trials.launch.xml map:=<name> qual:=true

  It adds three nodes that watch the run rather than drive it. Laps are counted
  from the first "2D Pose Estimate", the particle filter is re-seeded every
  relocalize_every_n_laps laps, and the speed schedule steps up once:

      laps 1..qual_switch_lap    scaling 0.5, t_clip_min 1.05
      after that                 scaling 0.6, t_clip_min 1.10

  Every number is an argument, and every one is also live on the running node:

      ros2 param set /qual_scheduler switch_lap 12
      ros2 param set /qual_scheduler phase2_scaling 0.65
      ros2 param set /auto_relocalizer relocalize_every_n_laps 5
      ros2 service call /qual_scheduler/reapply std_srvs/srv/Trigger

  The schedule works by setting sector_tuner's own Sector*.scaling and
  Sector*.t_clip_min, which is what a human dragging the rqt sliders does, so
  the map's speed_scaling.yaml is never written and a run left half-finished
  leaves nothing behind. Killing any of the three nodes mid-run is safe - the
  car keeps driving on whatever sector_tuner last had.

  RACELINE was formerly called GB_TRACK; see state_machine/states_types.py.

  No node started here publishes a transform. The chain is unchanged from
  localization.launch.xml - the racecar URDF through robot_state_publisher for
  everything rigid, the EKF for map -> odom - and the controller and state
  machine only read frames, never claim them. The two they name are
  parameters (base_frame, laser_frame), so they follow the URDF's
  ego_racecar/* rather than a bare base_link/laser.

  WHY THE RACELINE'S START POSE MATTERS HERE, not just in generation: the
  speed sectors are waypoint index ranges, and the state machine turns the
  car's Frenet s into an index with `s / waypoints_dist`. That only lines up
  because raceline_generator puts waypoint 0 at the start pose and we define
  sector 0 to begin there too. Regenerate the raceline from a different start
  pose and every sector boundary in speed_scaling.yaml moves with it - the
  numbers stay valid, they just point somewhere else on the track. Sectors
  also never wrap past the end for the same reason, which is what lets the
  zone test be a plain `start <= idx <= end`.

  THE JOYSTICK IS THE BRAKE. simple_mux starts in neither mode and only passes
  commands through once a button is held:

      LB (4)  human  - your stick drives the car
      RB (5)  auto   - the controller drives the car

  Release both and the mux publishes zero from the next tick - it hands control
  back rather than letting the last command stand. A source that goes stale is
  dropped the same way, so a controller crash or a joystick dropout both end in
  a commanded stop rather than the last speed repeating.

  joy_teleop is NOT started here - the mux reads /joy itself, and running both
  would put two things on the same output. The joystick DRIVER is still started:
  without it nothing publishes /joy, the mux never sees a button, and the car
  cannot be moved at all.

  Before the first autonomous run:
    - App Settings / General in the VESC: Timeout Brake Current is 0, so the
      VESC coasts instead of braking when commands stop arriving. That is the
      backup for the mux itself dying or the USB coming out, so it wants a real
      current. Timeout is 1000 ms, which is 5 m of travel at the joystick
      ceiling before anything happens.
    - Sector0.scaling starts at 0.5 and the ceiling is now 1.0. Raise it live
      once the line looks right, do not start there.
  Verified already: Motor Current Max Brake -60 A and Battery Current Max Regen
  -60 A, so commanded decelerations do reach the motor.
-->
<launch>
  <arg name="map" description="Map name (folder under stack_master/maps/)"/>
  <arg name="localization" default="true"
       description="Bring up localisation too. false if it is already running."/>
  <arg name="obstacles" default="false"
       description="Static obstacle avoidance: perception + the spline planner"/>

  <!-- ===== race day ===== -->
  <arg name="relocalize" default="true"
       description="Count laps and re-seed the particle filter at the finish
                    line every N laps. On by default - it is what keeps the
                    particle cloud from spreading over a long run, and it costs
                    one message every few laps."/>
  <arg name="qual" default="false"
       description="Qualifying run: the two-phase speed schedule on top. Lap
                    counting and re-localisation are `relocalize` and come up
                    on their own."/>
  <arg name="qual_switch_lap" default="10"
       description="[laps] Phase 1 runs to the end of this lap, phase 2 after."/>
  <arg name="qual_phase1_scaling" default="0.5"/>
  <arg name="qual_phase1_t_clip_min" default="1.05"/>
  <arg name="qual_phase2_scaling" default="0.6"/>
  <arg name="qual_phase2_t_clip_min" default="1.10"/>
  <arg name="relocalize_every_n_laps" default="3"
       description="[laps] Re-seed the particle filter this often. 0 disables
                    it without taking the node out of the launch."/>
  <arg name="qual_hud" default="true"
       description="Draw lap / speed / phase / re-localisations as an RViz text
                    marker on /qual_hud. Read-only."/>
  <arg name="qual_hud_frame" default="ego_racecar/base_link"
       description="Frame the overlay is drawn in. The car's frame makes it
                    follow the car; use map to pin it somewhere fixed."/>
  <arg name="qual_hud_height" default="0.8"
       description="[m] How far above the frame's origin the text sits."/>
  <arg name="qual_hud_text_size" default="0.28"
       description="[m] Cap height of the overlay text."/>

  <arg name="joy_max_speed" default="5.0"
       description="[m/s] full stick on the manual override. The controller is
                    capped separately, by global_limit in speed_scaling.yaml."/>
  <arg name="use_sim_time" default="false"/>
  <arg name="rviz" default="false"/>
  <arg name="foxglove" default="false"/>

  <!-- Derived, not a second switch: looking at obstacles and having something
       to publish them are the same decision. -->
  <let name="timetrials_only" value="true"  unless="$(var obstacles)"/>
  <let name="timetrials_only" value="false" if="$(var obstacles)"/>

  <let name="config_dir" value="$(find-pkg-share stack_master)/config/car"/>

  <!-- ===== everything below the driving logic ===== -->
  <group if="$(var localization)">
    <include file="$(find-pkg-share stack_master)/launch/localization.launch.xml">
      <arg name="map" value="$(var map)"/>
      <arg name="rviz" value="$(var rviz)"/>
      <arg name="foxglove" value="$(var foxglove)"/>
      <arg name="use_sim_time" value="$(var use_sim_time)"/>
      <!-- simple_mux reads /joy itself; joy_teleop would be a second publisher
           on the drive topic. This switches off joy_teleop only - the joy
           driver is a separate argument and stays on. -->
      <arg name="teleop" value="false"/>
    </include>
  </group>

  <!-- ===== sector parameters ===== -->
  <!-- Not optional here: the state machine blocks at startup waiting for
       /global_waypoints_scaled and /global_waypoints/overtaking, and these two
       are what publish them. Both read their yaml from the map folder, so the
       map must have been through raceline_generator. -->
  <let name="map_dir" value="$(find-pkg-share stack_master)/maps/$(var map)"/>
  <node pkg="sector_tuner" exec="sector_tuner" name="sector_tuner" output="screen">
    <param from="$(var map_dir)/speed_scaling.yaml"/>
    <param name="use_sim_time" value="$(var use_sim_time)"/>
  </node>
  <node pkg="sector_tuner" exec="ot_interpolator" name="ot_interpolator" output="screen">
    <param from="$(var map_dir)/ot_sectors.yaml"/>
    <param name="use_sim_time" value="$(var use_sim_time)"/>
  </node>

  <!-- ===== the racing brain ===== -->
  <node pkg="state_machine" exec="state_machine" name="state_machine" output="screen">
    <param from="$(var config_dir)/state_machine_params.yaml"/>
    <!-- Where to read racecar_f110.ini, veh_dyn_info/ and the sector yamls.
         Passed in because the node must not depend on stack_master: this
         launch belongs to stack_master, and the dependency would be a cycle. -->
    <param name="config_dir" value="$(var config_dir)"/>
    <param name="map_dir" value="$(var map_dir)"/>
    <param name="timetrials_only" value="$(var timetrials_only)"/>
    <param name="sim" value="$(var use_sim_time)"/>
    <param name="map" value="$(var map)"/>
    <param name="use_sim_time" value="$(var use_sim_time)"/>
  </node>

  <!-- ===== the controller ===== -->
  <node pkg="controller" exec="controller_manager" name="controller_manager" output="screen">
    <param from="$(var config_dir)/controller.yaml"/>
    <param name="save_yaml_path" value="$(var config_dir)/controller.yaml"/>
    <param name="sim" value="$(var use_sim_time)"/>
    <param name="use_sim_time" value="$(var use_sim_time)"/>
    <!-- The VESC's IMU, under the name the controller expects. -->
    <remap from="/imu/data" to="/vesc/sensors/imu/raw"/>
  </node>

  <!-- ===== static obstacle avoidance ===== -->
  <group if="$(var obstacles)">
    <!-- map is passed because the grid on-track test loads that map's
         image; on_track_mode:=frenet in perception.yaml makes it unused. -->
    <node pkg="perception" exec="detect" name="detect" output="screen">
      <param from="$(var config_dir)/perception.yaml"/>
      <param name="map" value="$(var map)"/>
      <param name="use_sim_time" value="$(var use_sim_time)"/>
    </node>
    <node pkg="perception" exec="tracking_node" name="tracking" output="screen">
      <param from="$(var config_dir)/perception.yaml"/>
      <param name="use_sim_time" value="$(var use_sim_time)"/>
    </node>

    <!-- spline, not spline_planner's dynamic_avoidance_node: that one only
         plans while /ot_section_check says yes, and nothing says yes on a map
         without overtaking sectors. Both publish the same topic, so only one
         may run. -->
    <node pkg="spline" exec="spline_node" name="spline_node" output="screen">
      <param from="$(var config_dir)/perception.yaml"/>
      <param name="use_sim_time" value="$(var use_sim_time)"/>
      <remap from="/planner/avoidance/otwpnts" to="/planner/avoidance/static_otwpnts"/>
    </node>
  </group>

  <!-- ===== qualifying ===== -->
  <!-- Three nodes, none of which the driving stack depends on: kill any of
       them mid-run and the car keeps driving on whatever sector_tuner last
       had. They are only in the launch so that starting a qualifying run is
       one command rather than four terminals.

       lap_analyser      /car_state/odom_frenet -> lap_data, one per lap
       relocalizer       lap_data -> /initialpose every N laps
       qual_scheduler    lap_data -> sector_tuner's scaling and t_clip_min

       The lap boundary is the FIRST "2D Pose Estimate" of the run, not
       waypoint 0, so lap 1 starts where the car is placed. The relocalizer
       republishes /initialpose to re-seed the particle filter and lap_analyser
       ignores those - only the first one moves the line. -->
  <!-- Lap counting and re-seeding, on by default. Both are cheap - the
       relocalizer subscribes to lap_data and publishes one pose every N laps -
       and the cloud spreading over a run is the failure they exist to stop.
       lap_analyser is here because it publishes the lap_data the other one
       counts, not for the lap times. -->
  <group if="$(var relocalize)">
    <node pkg="lap_analyser" exec="lap_analyser" name="lap_analyser" output="screen">
      <param name="lap_start_from_initialpose" value="true"/>
      <param name="use_sim_time" value="$(var use_sim_time)"/>
    </node>

    <node pkg="lap_analyser" exec="relocalizer" name="auto_relocalizer" output="screen">
      <param name="relocalize_every_n_laps" value="$(var relocalize_every_n_laps)"/>
      <param name="use_sim_time" value="$(var use_sim_time)"/>
    </node>
  </group>

  <!-- The speed schedule alone. It needs lap_data, so it needs relocalize on
       too - which it is, by default. -->
  <group if="$(var qual)">
    <node pkg="lap_analyser" exec="qual_scheduler" name="qual_scheduler" output="screen">
      <param name="switch_lap" value="$(var qual_switch_lap)"/>
      <param name="phase1_scaling" value="$(var qual_phase1_scaling)"/>
      <param name="phase1_t_clip_min" value="$(var qual_phase1_t_clip_min)"/>
      <param name="phase2_scaling" value="$(var qual_phase2_scaling)"/>
      <param name="phase2_t_clip_min" value="$(var qual_phase2_t_clip_min)"/>
      <param name="use_sim_time" value="$(var use_sim_time)"/>
    </node>

    <!-- Read-only overlay: lap, speed, phase and re-localisations as one
         RViz text marker on /qual_hud. Rides in the car's frame so it
         follows the car instead of sitting at the map origin. -->
    <node pkg="lap_analyser" exec="qual_hud" name="qual_hud" output="screen" if="$(var qual_hud)">
      <param name="frame_id" value="$(var qual_hud_frame)"/>
      <param name="offset_z" value="$(var qual_hud_height)"/>
      <param name="text_height" value="$(var qual_hud_text_size)"/>
      <param name="use_sim_time" value="$(var use_sim_time)"/>
    </node>
  </group>

  <!-- ===== command arbitration ===== -->
  <!-- The only thing that reaches the VESC. Holding LB gives the stick control,
       RB gives it to the controller, neither means no command at all. -->
  <node pkg="stack_master" exec="simple_mux_node.py" name="simple_mux" output="screen">
    <param name="in_topic"  value="/vesc/high_level/ackermann_cmd"/>
    <param name="out_topic" value="/vesc/ackermann_cmd"/>
    <param name="joy_max_speed" value="$(var joy_max_speed)"/>
    <!-- [rad] the car's measured steering limit. The mux default is 0.4,
         which asks for more than the car has. -->
    <param name="joy_max_steer" value="0.2954"/>
    <!-- The mux carries its own copy of the servo calibration for its steering
         clip. Point it at the real numbers so the two cannot drift apart;
         config/car/vesc.yaml is where they are kept. -->
    <param name="steering_angle_to_servo_gain"   value="-1.1"/>
    <param name="steering_angle_to_servo_offset" value="0.445"/>
    <param name="servo_min" value="0.12"/>
    <param name="servo_max" value="0.77"/>
    <param name="use_estop" value="false"/>
    <param name="sim" value="$(var use_sim_time)"/>
    <param name="use_sim_time" value="$(var use_sim_time)"/>
  </node>
</launch>
